{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When there is a dispute between men</b>, according to the view of our Sages (Makkot 13a) that lashes apply to those liable for negative commandments — what does the dispute between the two men have to do with it? One who eats nevelah in his house receives lashes, and likewise one who plants his field or vineyard with kil'ayim, and one who lies with any of the women forbidden by a negative commandment, and all other negative commandments. Moreover, what is the meaning of 'and they shall acquit the righteous'? Therefore they interpreted it with reference to conspiring witnesses (Makkot 2b): Scripture says — when there is a dispute between two men and they approach the court, and they are judged according to the testimony of two witnesses as they were commanded, and afterward 'they shall acquit the righteous and condemn the wicked' — not according to the first judgment. 'And it shall be, if the wicked man deserves to be beaten' — in a case where we cannot fulfill in him 'and you shall do to him as he schemed to do to his brother,' this being a case where they testified about him that he is the son of a divorcee, or a slave, or a mamzer, or where they testified about him that he transgressed one of the negative commandments — he shall receive forty lashes. And it is also possible that there may be a dispute between men and he receives lashes through it, such as where he wounded him with damage worth less than a perutah, or where he cursed his fellow with the Name, or where he took a pledge from him of items used for preparing food and the like. And Scripture speaks of the common case, for the disputant brings him to court and through him he is lashed. The reason for forty lashes, according to the midrash (Tanhuma Yashan, Bemidbar 28), is that because he transgressed the Torah, which was given in forty days, and caused death to himself, who was formed over forty days, he shall receive forty lashes and thereby discharge his punishment. The matter of yibum, its secret, and halitzah and its reason, I have already mentioned (Genesis 38:8)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The citation 'Tanhuma Yashan, Bemidbar 28' is resolved from the Hebrew abbreviation; the specific section number is uncertain and follows the standard printed edition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:25.198089458Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}